        "title": "A variety of writing systems",
        "text": "Writing is something we do every day without a second thought. Yet linguists disagree about how to define the activity, and about how best to describe some of the world's writing systems.\n\nWriting seems to have developed independently at different times in several parts of the world, among them Mexico and Central America, China, and Mesopotamia (present-day Iraq). One of the earliest writing systems developed to record ancient Egyptian about 5,000 years ago. The signs are called hieroglyphs, and they are of three types. Some represent ideas or objects from the real world, such as beetle (a type of insect) and swallow (a type of bird); others indicate sounds; and the third group are used to tell apart two words that are otherwise identical. Carve and retreat, for example, are represented by the same hieroglyph, but a knife symbol is added to show when the first meaning is intended, and a pair of legs to show the second. Some hieroglyphs are pictures, such as a drawing of a beetle or a swallow; some were originally pictures and became more abstract; and others are symbols. Because of its complexity, the Egyptian system was far better for communication than earlier systems, which could express only a limited range of meanings. Hieroglyphs remained in use in Egypt for about 3,000 years (for some of that time alongside other writing systems) before they were replaced by an alphabetic system.\n\nChinese has been written for nearly 4,000 years. Like many Egyptian hieroglyphs, its signs were originally pictures, and gradually became more abstract. The writing system consists of characters that represent words, sections of words, or ideas. Chinese characters are used throughout the country, and also in the unrelated languages of Japanese, Korean and, until the twentieth century, Vietnamese. They can be compared with mathematical symbols such as = or +, which have the same meaning in all languages but represent different words. There are thousands of Chinese characters to learn, and they take a long time to draw and to type: a traditional Chinese typewriter can have over a thousand keys, and even a skilled typist is only expected to type about eleven words a minute.\n\nA completely new development in writing appeared in the Middle East about 3,700 years ago, when Egyptian hieroglyphs were well established. This was the North Semitic alphabet, which developed in Palestine and Syria. The Phoenicians, a trading nation living on the coast of modern Lebanon, adapted it to form their own alphabet. This in turn spread into northern Africa, where it became the writing system of the Arabs, and north-west to Greece. The Greek letters were changed further to become the Cyrillic alphabets of Russia and part of the Balkans. The Romans adapted the letters into the alphabet that is still used for many languages, including all those of Western Europe and the written languages of North and South America, which are, of course, European in origin.\n\nAlphabets are the most adaptable of all writing systems. A small number of symbols that represent important sounds in a language, not pictures or ideas, can be combined in different ways to represent all the words of the language. While most alphabets contain between 20 and 30 letters, the smallest, used in the Solomon Islands, has only 11. Khmer, the official language of Cambodia, has the largest alphabet, with 74 letters. Alphabetic systems are very flexible and can easily be used on computers, so the Latin alphabet is used in both China and Japan alongside the traditional writing systems.\n\nAlthough alphabets are based on sound, spelling and pronunciation rarely match very closely. Finnish and Macedonian are among the most regular. English, however, is far more irregular, partly because its writing system has not kept up with changes in pronunciation over the centuries."
